  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nThe southwest flow will continue across the region today with an\nupper trough digging over the Pacific Northwest. As a disturbance\nlifts through the flow, afternoon showers over central Montana\nwill expand slowly eastward into the late afternoon and into\nnortheast Montana by the evening. Wind Gusts currently look to be\nthe main threat with 60+ mph showing up in the HREF Max Wind \nGusts. However these gusts appear to moderate out into the evening\nhours around the time they move into northeast Montana. SPC has a\nslight risk for severe thunderstorms over north central Montana \nwith a slight risk for the remainder of northeast Montana.\n\nTemperatures will remain warm through Friday with the continued \nsouthwest flow into Montana and ridge over the northern plains.\nHighs may reach the upper 90s in the southeast on Friday which\nlend to elevated Fire Weather concerns if wind gust can ramp up as\nwell. \n\nEarly next week as the upper ridge tilts westward and the west\ncoast trough closes off, another round of showers and\nthunderstorms is expected across the region. This is the second\nday for a strong precipitation total signal from Sunday night into\nMonday night. While these totals could still reduce in further\nforecasts, currently up to 1 inch is being teased at some\nlocations over these two periods. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nDeviations from NBM include a NPoP blend of CAMs late this\nafternoon through roughly 15Z Thursday morning to better capture\nconvective elements. Also, did minor tweaks to MinRH and hourly RH\nin order to maintain consistency with BYZ's Red Flag tweaks. \n\nThere is HIGH confidence in forecast through Friday. \n\nThere is MODERATE confidence Saturday to Monday with next wave of\nrain.\n\nThere is LOW confidence from Monday night onward.\n\n/GAH",
